{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('state.json'))\ni=[x for x in st['interpretations'] if x['id']=='2c75254c008c414ca5e88b385581c2ac-interpretation-0'][0]\nprint(i['rationale'])\n\\\" > /tmp/rat.txt 2>/dev/null || python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run/state.json'))\ni=[x for x in st['interpretations'] if x['id']=='2c75254c008c414ca5e88b385581c2ac-interpretation-0'][0]\nprint(i['rationale'])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: line 1: /tmp/rat.txt: Permission denied\nConclusion: violated, and the scoped defect is asserted. A configuration change whose log write fails is reported to the caller as failed, but the new configuration is nevertheless installed in the leader's in-memory latest configuration at the index the entry would have occupied. The node then treats that unstored configuration as authoritative for two separate decisions: whom it counts as a voter and whom it asks for votes. Evidence from receipt 0e6dd623c01e43a4b9e33cafc544ecf2 (completed, tests_passed, exit 0). Parsed index 0, the case event: two voters were bootstrapped, the leader's store failed exactly the configuration write, and afterwards latest_index_after_fail=3 with last_log_index_after_fail=2, while phantom_servers records the shrunken configuration containing only the leader itself. Parsed index 1, the result: remove_failed=true with remove_error 'injected store failure', latest_index_exceeds_log=true, re_elected=true, apply_ok=true, commit_index=4, leader_last_index=4 and peer_last_index=2, so commit_without_peer=true. Under the committed two-voter configuration a quorum is both members, so the command the client was told had committed at index 4 never reached a quorum of that configuration. Mechanism from source: appendConfigurationEntry dispatches the configuration entry and then, unconditionally, calls setLatestConfiguration with the new configuration at the dispatched index, reconfigures the commitment map and restarts replication (raft.go:1204-1244), while dispatchLogs' store-error path only answers the futures with the error and moves the node to follower (raft.go:1245-1296). quorumSize, electSelf and setupLeaderState all read that latest configuration (raft.go:1087-1100, 1999-2071, 456-468), and startStopReplication opens a stream to every configured server (raft.go:583-600), so the phantom configuration governs the election the node wins, the quorum it uses to commit, and the set of servers whose acknowledgements it collects. The restart phase of receipt 76bdaf3e107643d4a3ed1247faa52460 is withdrawn as evidence: that harness created a fresh in-memory transport for the restarted node without connecting it to the other member's transport, so the restarted node could not reach the peer at all, and its restarted_as_leader=false and unchanged peer log index are explained by the missing wiring. The target's own membership document states the condition this violates: a new configuration 'will be in affect as soon as it is appended to the log' (membership.md:15). In the failed-write case nothing was appended, so the condition the document sets for a configuration to take effect was never met, yet the node used the new voter set for its election and for committing. Shrink direction, four receipts: after a failed RemoveServer the phantom configuration contains only the leader and the later command commits with commit_without_peer=true in 0e6dd623c01e43a4b9e33cafc544ecf2 (parsed index 1), in the corrected restart revision adbbb5d29a08477b9dbbd7da187f7f26 (parsed index 1), in the shrink case of the two-direction control 99292afefb9d4308957a0e2d150bf11d (parsed index 1) and in the clean-copy repeat e7a4c652bb2b4273972d661adccd41e9 (parsed index 1), with the leader and removed follower roles covered in both assignments across those receipts. Recovery after the lone commit was observed in two of them (adbbb5d29a08477b9dbbd7da187f7f26 parsed index 2, e7a4c652bb2b4273972d661adccd41e9 parsed index 2): the restarted node led again and the peer's log reached the index the lone commit had occupied. Grown direction, now decisive: receipt f64ce69f57134681b38e73bed2fd137e (completed, tests_passed, exit 0) runs three real nodes, bootstraps {A,B} as the committed voter set and starts C with no configuration, so the membership guards in requestVote that test a non-empty server list do not reject the leader (raft.go:1696-1707). Parsed index 0, the reachable case event: AddVoter('C') failed with the injected store error, latest_index_after_fail=3 against last_log_index_after_fail=2, and phantom_servers records {A,B,C} with three voters. Parsed index 1, the reachable case result: after the leader was partitioned from B and left connected to C, re_elected=true, apply_ok=true, commit_index=4 with leader_last_index=4 and new_server_last_index=4, while committed_peer_last_index=2, so committed_peer_has_entry=false and commit_without_committed_voter=true. The leader therefore regained office on its own vote plus C's, and reached the commit quorum of two with its own acknowledgement plus C's, while the single committed voter other than itself never received the entry. Parsed index 2, the control case event: the same failed write left the same three-voter phantom configuration. Parsed index 3, the control result: with both B and C unreachable, re_elected=false, apply_ok=false with the apply refused as 'node is not the leader', commit_index=2 and new_server_last_index=0, so no command committed. Cutting C is what removed the second vote and the second acknowledgement, which is what makes C the supplier of the support that produced the trigger's commit. Consequence under the exercised conditions: after a transient failure to store a configuration change, a node can adopt a voter set the rest of the cluster never accepted, win an election with the vote of a server that is not in the committed configuration, and report a client command committed while the committed voters hold different logs. The earlier statement that this needs a failed removal, and the earlier statement that a grown phantom quorum necessarily retains a committed majority, are both refuted: the grown configuration {A,B,C} admits {A,C}, and when that pair was reachable it produced exactly the unsupported commit the shrink direction produces.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
